        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
        ":question/problem": "What is the value of ${\\int }_{e/2}^{{e}^{2}/2}\\frac{2}{3x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2x).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2x}\\cdot 2=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e/2$ | ${e}^{2}/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e/2}^{{e}^{2}/2}\\frac{2}{3x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{e/2}^{{e}^{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|u|){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612-\\operatorname{ln}\u20611) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u20612\\end{aligned}\n$$",
